ng a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88a2376c-dc19-5dd0-a77a-56366a05b81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113209} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a28036-b367-589e-9512-aab611b56f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113210} {:answer/id #uuid "d3998ecb-9d96-51bb-a80c-b98dba07a111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113211} {:answer/id #uuid "1afc57e2-95e9-5005-8244-a42d7d643df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113212} {:answer/id #uuid "64c56874-5776-54fe-a57b-179c6f752f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113213}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4e1aae67-1413-5c52-92c2-e130fc7ee0d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113208} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0c131ae-15e2-51d9-b981-699dd3822a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390580} {:answer/id #uuid "4abb149a-acd5-5b9b-b236-51b85d318980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390581} {:answer/id #uuid "ea956b89-f42b-52b5-a970-4a7cbdfbbbcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390582} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c1bdc4-e73b-5851-82fe-a8e4df60d98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390583} {:answer/id #uuid "ea082471-0a98-54a0-9c3f-4a9fcbd75fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1c1bdc4-e73b-5851-82fe-a8e4df60d98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390583} :answer-field/id #uuid "974b5aa7-9854-5922-a33e-647715b7b09b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20eb540b-84ee-5a09-81e9-e0f110dad1d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-29179" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={60}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={60}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={300}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{60}^{∘},-{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-60\\cdot (-300)=18\\,000\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21c9bca-5889-5378-95f5-dc19174253a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390587} {:answer/id #uuid "5637088d-2110-5861-a9e4-5418d97814d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390588} {:answer/id #uuid "98625043-9024-52ca-9b27-4d102460e169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390589} {:answer/id #uuid "a3127733-14dd-544e-9920-fd0e17095202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390590} {:answer/id #uuid "510c63c8-8440-585e-ac63-db0b2b6359b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98625043-9024-52ca-9b27-4d102460e169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390589} :answer-field/id #uuid "c557a89c-b4c6-5e67-988d-6c588aefa937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23dbd8da-da31-5df7-bb91-fad8d1a7bceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-29182" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={30}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={30}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/8c/8c6e3ae6bdbfb1c66aee719f77225453992fffe6f3c6e3dfdc3f1ff92c59863a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={330}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required dommain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={330}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{30}^{∘},-{330}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-30\\cdot (-330)=9\\,900\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba963ded-e9ff-5324-9d22-acb6b7a85e10" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285b16e3-c1a0-528c-aa3c-6503ddc7d9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295491} {:answer/id #uuid "87b96519-0b71-53f7-a2a8-38e23e3ed540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295998} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc605ec-311a-5e45-aa11-fa47080ee6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296021} {:answer/id #uuid "a73b3e93-a76c-5d8c-952d-0ca55c712a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296147} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc07055-c07b-591e-bb1f-6c27db9c5cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dc605ec-311a-5e45-aa11-fa47080ee6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296021} :answer-field/id #uuid "38ba5974-6a88-54e6-877e-8ae9daa987cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e82d568-df50-578f-b07c-119397489f57" :question/math-academy-id "q-29207" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8854eaba-96c7-5ea8-a071-7ff7c954f943" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Asymptotes of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8266d9da-29ef-5142-889c-67032e696941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295969} {:answer/id #uuid "8f36dd3b-18aa-5423-9a63-6be24cb45a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296030} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a3b5aa-10fe-59e1-8b60-91383fc7c7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296242} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3fe793-587a-5cb1-82ef-b60494e08387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296347} {:answer/id #uuid "f5728d81-5718-5a99-b836-93226b4b6cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9a3b5aa-10fe-59e1-8b60-91383fc7c7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296242} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d308903-8122-5b47-a276-3957b8fde22e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "493ac61e-d5aa-548d-a691-2dfa99e52431" :question/math-academy-id "q-29349" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-2,$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-2,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x).$ This gives:\n\n$$\n2x={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSolving this for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a258070e-5c3c-595e-81d6-3e0f9b9a5daf" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Mixed Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c28cb971-d849-577e-9682-a8890d4ae773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401930} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c91422-9ef9-5a21-a3cd-6168971db223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401931} {:answer/id #uuid "928ea34b-f94c-5914-8894-15d7edbe4d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401932} {:answer/id #uuid "129beb51-d7c5-5a45-86b1-53f54c6e7926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge x+2 \\\\ y<-x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401933} {:answer/id #uuid "1f5ea53c-6d9f-56de-99b0-c8f54f45ac23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y<-x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "928ea34b-f94c-5914-8894-15d7edbe4d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401932} :answer-field/id #uuid "210acf01-110f-53da-bf3c-8725abe1a7d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cb8229e-2a6a-582b-a6c3-627b88cf6816" :question/math-academy-id "q-29384" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a740aafbb9f74e2681506d3027f848fa92e7eea2bac3e08ccaf3fc7e0acf8fa1.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown in the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge -x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074992e8-e1fa-5890-859b-ab91c898befa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401937} {:answer/id #uuid "95aa0b04-7251-5fbf-b2eb-66bafc65e559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65.5\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401938} {:answer/id #uuid "336d2d1d-872d-56d4-a5f4-5bec78ef5250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401939} {:answer/id #uuid "35306f0e-0e84-5e47-a2f4-679a063c0acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401940} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f8d307-81e4-50be-9385-c254d6f22bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75.2\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35306f0e-0e84-5e47-a2f4-679a063c0acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401940} :answer-field/id #uuid "6827bd64-2698-5152-a010-779141f5c62e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "507fbe2b-2f95-5bfa-be32-3403810daadf" :question/math-academy-id "q-2939" :question/problem "![](images/92/9283bcc77ac8e63c1ba00f9a81e659bcbcf41bc98b94a1a0617b02feab71cf0a.png)\n\nCar $B$ is being driven due south toward Caltech at a speed of $60\\,\\text{km/h},$ while car $A$ is being driven due east away from Caltech. When car $B$ is $0.6\\,\\text{km}$ away from Caltech, car $A$ is $0.8\\,\\text{km}$ from Caltech. If the distance between the two vehicles is increasing at a rate of $20\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is the speed of car $A?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/6e/6e7ef0d4665710a31c1c601048498f20ad6b3099769c1d140320074805964ec7.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Caltech and car $A$,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Caltech and car $B$,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two cars.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=0.8\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=0.6\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {0.8}^{2}+{0.6}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =1\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-60\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=20\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0.8)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(0.6)(-60) & =2(1)(20) \\\\ 1.6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =40+72 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{112}{1.6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car $A$ is $70\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc9e1e5-11ce-551d-97de-0eca949cc8ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1350km/h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062288} {:answer/id #uuid "c6cc7f50-9e41-5b9f-864d-0e28be15a521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1000km/h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062289} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8734e2-d020-5c3d-a702-dca63ef20d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "975km/h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062290} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ff4680-bdaf-5954-b0ff-5d1660a2bbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1250km/h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062291} {:answer/id #uuid "270d7699-bf39-50bb-b2d0-bfe7bfa68dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "750m/h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062292}] :answer-field/id #uuid "940ab7e7-9338-589e-a3c0-36ebb036a673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062287} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e61c15b-79c7-5166-b178-3747e17866dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401943} {:answer/id #uuid "b14cf7b0-2299-5e5f-b5c6-d5c85c148877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,250\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401944} {:answer/id #uuid "b8011803-44bb-5708-a342-7bae100244be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,350\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401945} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f10350-a4ef-5fe3-aef8-799220547f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "975\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401946} {:answer/id #uuid "062254bd-0f6e-5f77-8e3b-5a1990d9eb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "750\\,\\text{m/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b14cf7b0-2299-5e5f-b5c6-d5c85c148877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,250\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401944} :answer-field/id #uuid "81478755-4be2-5ae2-9535-e8dd0fd9f860" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "216f383a-6fa0-5f0b-b2c6-22682df9e591" :question/math-academy-id "q-2941" :question/problem "A rocket that is launched vertically is tracked by a radar station located on\nthe ground $3\\,\\text{km}$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the rocket at the instant when its distance from the radar station is $5\\,\\text{km}$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $1\\,000\\,\\text{km/h}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/7a/7af033f137fd76e5e3003926a598e695f4e0852b11dc27dfa84d58040144972b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the radar station and the launch point,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the launch point and the rocket, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the radar station and the rocket.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $y$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,000\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the radar station and the launch point ($x$) remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)(0)+2(4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(5)(1\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{10\\,000}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,250\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical speed of the rocket is $1\\,250\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fabd8865-cc14-508a-aa81-4ba147f7f8d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Zeros of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64fab536-011b-5495-af75-b22e97ee61ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295842} {:answer/id #uuid "87e9cce5-fd91-5d27-9de1-74ed14eca40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296000} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e90731-2073-5376-a7f3-d74585469490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296130} {:answer/id #uuid "d917947c-0694-5b63-b083-5660cef98161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296412} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2df703-693b-5e97-a3f0-fa4ca9c37fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64fab536-011b-5495-af75-b22e97ee61ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295842} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae41aa8c-95fb-5956-b88c-4aa3ce7bb65c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97f191d4-6a4f-5a4f-8505-bbd4c324e609" :question/math-academy-id "q-29438" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx-\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b53db17c-c360-5669-8f88-e93f4bdbd8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401950} {:answer/id #uuid "25dcd0ff-82e2-5596-97c0-808147eeda5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401951} {:answer/id #uuid "c06a013e-9247-5fe8-9462-4797376bce04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401952} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5c203d-08c8-5beb-ba1e-76f154f20aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401953} {:answer/id #uuid "12121f61-eccf-5393-b245-c6404140579b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25dcd0ff-82e2-5596-97c0-808147eeda5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401951} :answer-field/id #uuid "8895f00a-eea4-5d81-b69d-a26ada8cb409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c11092b-9e53-51e1-b299-c5287bf677e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-29477" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3$\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fabd8865-cc14-508a-aa81-4ba147f7f8d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Zeros of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53c921c9-46e0-5bef-95de-0f5b588ebca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295749} {:answer/id #uuid "85d07a4f-56af-578a-a679-6e632e293884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295983} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7e58fb-f78c-5c59-97d6-77d4f59a186a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296016} {:answer/id #uuid "ab14ced1-375e-5455-909c-7a3ced2bd998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296164} {:answer/id #uuid "ee73340c-4d12-5b82-9ec4-7452a202940f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab14ced1-375e-5455-909c-7a3ced2bd998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296164} :answer-field/id #uuid "79362fdd-d341-5e89-bee7-6cb695806517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "670b4725-4f5f-5e5b-81ee-7bebf6dbdf85" :question/math-academy-id "q-29556" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064a647e-d06a-50bb-9cbc-0f17389eca42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295298} {:answer/id #uuid "1066d92d-62c9-5dad-8b4c-dbbe71386d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295362} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3a0291-6ad9-5566-9fd5-0de08933b4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295630} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ba8e5-5fce-5a75-b9a3-f9b2aab87da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2ff4e9-ee34-51e6-b267-06c09d19013f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ba8e5-5fce-5a75-b9a3-f9b2aab87da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1618392-44be-533c-81a8-efd84eeee3ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5a3a079-0ec8-5b33-84d2-568517288149" :question/math-academy-id "q-29608" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\n- The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$\n- $tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+2n\\pi ).$\n- The range of the function is $[-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+2n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi +n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )=tan⁡(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The range of $y=tan⁡x$ is $(-\\infty ,+\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115185 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c9c0f6-034c-5d9c-935a-ca27dec1a648" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35051668-a56c-5024-9510-5bb50d0360fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,741" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395806} {:answer/id #uuid "9c07c6a6-5b40-55a4-9250-674b5ab51617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,731" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395807} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4cbb58-b903-5380-9c08-172acb79c3a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,631" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395808} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbfb4e9-a306-5d6f-a040-1f5b9ed69042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,851" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395809} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3be72d-8de9-5e24-a778-ee5537402bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,561" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35051668-a56c-5024-9510-5bb50d0360fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,741" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395806} :answer-field/id #uuid "4440a234-4417-5c7c-aab8-361ac22515f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fa4866c-44a3-53be-9ea4-1b3c073da31b" :question/math-academy-id "q-29664" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{4}{3})=(-53.130\\,1…)^{∘}≈-{53.130}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-{53.130}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-{53.130}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+x={180}^{∘}-{53.130}^{∘}={126.870}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{53.130}^{∘},{126.870}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n-53.130\\cdot 126.870≈-6\\,741\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1113b2b6-ac10-5599-9e94-5bbed95e32a5" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144a6036-1a43-5c55-bd4d-8c3b921ab81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295382} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4bc189-56e8-506b-afae-8c32583fd169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295423} {:answer/id #uuid "4809f8aa-d435-582b-a813-64757ceea05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295678} {:answer/id #uuid "598e8e48-0407-5a12-b4c7-8a90c76b22d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295784} {:answer/id #uuid "addb0989-293c-52f5-817b-1eac8428806e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "144a6036-1a43-5c55-bd4d-8c3b921ab81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295382} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a2dd69-37ef-5e8e-92ea-d1b66a49d028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "380886ef-0fbf-54bc-9579-e4e21dd4f7ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-29673" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/2+1}}{-\\frac{5}{2}+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{3}}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}-\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{1})^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078597 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9d9c4e-2687-5a00-b777-4684cc0de27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295338} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffdef35-a30a-5f30-99b8-4a995eac8ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295632} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bb394a-fe2f-5d30-b8bc-9546189fbccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296144} {:answer/id #uuid "c0eee9c9-1fa3-56af-a61c-95a972738e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296285} {:answer/id #uuid "e36988ba-6cfe-5f34-b0a1-f31a0c88f51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0eee9c9-1fa3-56af-a61c-95a972738e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296285} :answer-field/id #uuid "e17078ea-5afe-5ed2-9a45-a07ca7d4a2b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ee241ee-6c9b-581f-8e3e-00ecaf1e5967" :question/math-academy-id "q-29734" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-9, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{x}{5}-5, & x\\ge 5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}(x-9)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{5}^{10}(\\frac{x}{5}-5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-9x)|}_{0}^{5}+{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{10}-5x)|}_{5}^{10} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(5)^{2}}{2}-9(5)-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2}+9(0)]+[\\frac{(10)^{2}}{10}-5(10)-\\frac{(5)^{2}}{10}+5(5)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{25}{2}-45]+[10-50-\\frac{25}{10}+25] \\\\  & =-50.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078597 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021675ce-f69e-5717-beee-62f1b55fe9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295272} {:answer/id #uuid "3e46952e-9cec-5cb5-af26-701cae6d5879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295626} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcb4361-9b61-51c7-9aaf-35ffbda245cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295875} {:answer/id #uuid "e3676b27-8ef7-5622-91a9-2bca7ef2dc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296478} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2022c7-8746-55e0-8d32-73482f73ca68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3676b27-8ef7-5622-91a9-2bca7ef2dc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296478} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c909a9-c1e8-56c3-a557-6fc2fdb9e1a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "598039da-8879-5b3c-ab71-e359f8b00731" :question/math-academy-id "q-29742" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x-9, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-1, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(3x-9)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}(\\frac{x}{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}-9x)|}_{0}^{3}+{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-x)|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{3(3)^{2}}{2}-9(3)-\\frac{3(0)^{2}}{2}+9(0)]+[\\frac{(6)^{2}}{6}-6-\\frac{(3)^{2}}{6}+3] \\\\  & =[\\frac{27}{2}-27]+[6-6-\\frac{3}{2}+3] \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064196 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67bbd172-1940-5a1e-82f8-ffc73058f009" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6f3f8e-0c5f-5742-981c-efe44d651035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295321} {:answer/id #uuid "7306755f-204c-5784-9675-85e4c40d4fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295896} {:answer/id #uuid "97ff1ca8-cab0-5552-a6be-a28c36723d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296080} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbdd0be-cf59-5728-817c-107e52a30f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{189}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296104} {:answer/id #uuid "ce989bf2-91f9-5cfb-894e-5fff41cce741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7306755f-204c-5784-9675-85e4c40d4fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295896} :answer-field/id #uuid "29935fac-8196-589a-b966-f9c10dd6483e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ea9ad4a-c4e1-5eef-bc95-8f5cd98ebf22" :question/math-academy-id "q-29744" :question/problem "Evaluate  ${\\int }_{-5}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1}{|}_{-5}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-5}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-5)^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{125}{3} \\\\  & =63.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c5a147-0100-5f4b-a221-25b8674b2115" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Integral of a Sum of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac468f7-459a-524a-906d-0b77ce69c347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295509} {:answer/id #uuid "3866e65f-5a6b-5139-971d-78279fa95a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295585} {:answer/id #uuid "3d776cfc-e9b5-5e0f-a90c-6c72a5495162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{268}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295619} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdc102e-c773-5605-aa07-bee1b412e617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295706} {:answer/id #uuid "d67385d1-7806-5881-a583-0cce0325b5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{268}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d67385d1-7806-5881-a583-0cce0325b5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{268}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296394} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e2b20e-f764-56d3-b81b-879a2eeacf48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "953eaea1-7562-55f0-8e7b-723ba8d5e0ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-29780" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{4}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{4}(2{x}^{3/2}+3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of three integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2{x}^{3/2}+3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}{|}_{0}^{4}+3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{4}+x\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/2}{|}_{0}^{4}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4}+x\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}({4}^{5/2}-{0}^{5/2})+\\frac{3}{2}({4}^{2}-{0}^{2})+(4-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 32+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 16+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{5}+28 \\\\  & =\\frac{268}{5}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155da407-c8ba-5fa2-9127-413d6bff8d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3ln⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295391} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca76ff5-0227-56f5-94be-d80b462a5d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{ln⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295528} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5899f6-d895-5413-bfee-0587b9cf1149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295802} {:answer/id #uuid "705c2710-b928-5586-a10e-67ac7d7acb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} {:answer/id #uuid "bd595728-5d66-5fce-842a-829a9bf9e495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-3}{ln⁡|x|}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "705c2710-b928-5586-a10e-67ac7d7acb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6439f4c-9689-5960-aa2c-ca05e0a5ed1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3f7435c-4593-5eb7-979c-b6475dc0ac4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-29831" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-3$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9f3ccb-57a5-54e7-9651-cd8e38e071bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡(5K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295330} {:answer/id #uuid "137963fa-e7bf-5b99-b97d-16a6e2b83b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295376} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba78d99-70e5-5576-9ab3-f6f5d63e5557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6cacd1-8561-54df-98e0-e7424109595d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296168} {:answer/id #uuid "e35e2f64-0c17-5dfe-a27c-81e2452e3e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba78d99-70e5-5576-9ab3-f6f5d63e5557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} :answer-field/id #uuid "c450e82f-bb3f-51ae-bbf9-78580de51634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eec65b66-7ead-5d6e-b179-5aacd453b9d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-29834" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $-\\frac{5}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-\\frac{5}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c865ec2-5bed-5892-966b-a586097be923" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298a44ce-4517-57c9-8784-5136e6d9c6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}{x}^{11/3}+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295500} {:answer/id #uuid "2e55410d-693f-527e-b63c-efdd8027016a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}{x}^{4/3}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295534} {:answer/id #uuid "91074ff5-3842-537b-b69c-20abfee3093d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296046} {:answer/id #uuid "ae484638-462d-55ee-b006-7e7f7e36be1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}{x}^{7/3}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296183} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b38dca-ffa5-5659-aa91-113d55a9cced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6b38dca-ffa5-5659-aa91-113d55a9cced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296395} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4ea97da-7b64-5d03-a1a8-9448f2925fe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9a35970-b3a0-5bae-b863-30b3731d8d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-29885" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the sum rule and get\n\n$$\n\\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\sqrt{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, using the power rule in the first term, and taking out the coefficient in the second term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\sqrt{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2/3+1}}{\\frac{2}{3}+1}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c865ec2-5bed-5892-966b-a586097be923" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abba1e8-07a5-5982-8931-920e2b54ed11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295324} {:answer/id #uuid "14c3a82a-71bb-5b22-a1d7-5d3ffaa433a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} {:answer/id #uuid "218a6eff-a0a2-5775-878d-fb334d854a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295454} {:answer/id #uuid "5203ee4a-c762-565e-a1e7-1c414c5d786d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295738} {:answer/id #uuid "e96a5e23-083b-5d3f-bddc-50fd9a0aed8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14c3a82a-71bb-5b22-a1d7-5d3ffaa433a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} :answer-field/id #uuid "65380cf5-c8c6-595a-9584-e99d721414af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77e80b12-44f5-5ee6-b586-d6d7898a0c32" :question/math-academy-id "q-29915" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1}$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the exponents and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2-3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115183 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab6f005d-5267-58e5-84bf-a80272331a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395813} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3a07e3-abf9-52a3-90a9-53487e034b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,641" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395814} {:answer/id #uuid "ce65e29e-1b9d-54dc-94af-379795c62563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,741" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395815} {:answer/id #uuid "198aafc0-07ef-5e53-9034-41f2a9658b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,746" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395816} {:answer/id #uuid "21c0594d-dee0-5fde-9687-2438c3bda33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,764" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce65e29e-1b9d-54dc-94af-379795c62563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,741" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395815} :answer-field/id #uuid "d68ef59f-9eeb-59d5-b977-da3a30f3eafc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa37b599-4ad5-59f0-a1aa-7677947192cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-29938" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{5}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{4}{5})=(53.130\\,1…)^{∘}≈{53.130}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${53.130}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={53.130}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={53.130}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={53.130}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/60/605af77803fb027c09833b50801fe4b8e6a42a2b623af94a732d0f2f222f9f4a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{53.130}^{∘} \\\\  & ={126.870}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x={53.130}^{∘},{126.870}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈53.130\\cdot 126.870≈6\\,741.\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7614a05-f221-5fda-b491-6893f44a1a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with Multiple Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c4f1d16-78b3-5045-8bb5-5579fec4543a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295613} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3602d9-f3c5-5344-8c7b-288df52749cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11{y}^{2}}{k+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295709} {:answer/id #uuid "93b20eed-4388-595a-84d8-977910934218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{k+4}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296058} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7ed919-742c-5a4c-b988-a4265e3f6f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{44(k+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296162} {:answer/id #uuid "e8492be0-a6b9-5d09-afa1-2e0f3bbeb2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8492be0-a6b9-5d09-afa1-2e0f3bbeb2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296502} :answer-field/id #uuid "17d9d487-4676-539c-9b1c-0c3a024bdb73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "964f86e1-4f13-55f4-a1ab-8caf3da2db2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-29970" :question/problem "If $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ then what is $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\nY=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{121y}{2(k+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2(k+2)\\cdot (k+2)}{11y\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot 11y}{2(k+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2(k+2)}\\cdot (k+2)}{\\cancel{11y}\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot \\cancel{11y}}{\\cancel{2(k+2)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 11 & = \\\\ \\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d78ba1c-5297-5540-afdc-d419d33e0cf7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9253c945-96c0-5170-ab31-77a3ad0f24d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 7, x_{1} + x_{2} = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066485} {:answer/id #uuid "a2710526-3439-5d45-a3cb-b94809df5d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 9, x_{1} + x_{2} = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066486} {:answer/id #uuid "b5eac8af-989a-5812-82cd-e6e80a244860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 7, x_{1} + x_{2} =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066487} {:answer/id #uuid "cec6266a-38e0-5082-ae2c-0c20a9b251e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 7, x_{1} + x_{2} = 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066488} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b0d285-5968-53b9-b5b2-00fcaf5e3048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q =-11x_{1} + x_{2} = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066489}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d792d360-b68f-53ea-add1-82615c19183b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066484} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76d7ebef-b52d-5f30-ac74-d49866c8f6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401956} {:answer/id #uuid "d00f8b18-cb37-5c31-961a-3438330ad683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401957} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0444d5-a369-5e49-89bd-c5ebc8c0237a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-11\\,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401958} {:answer/id #uuid "44f7e400-b932-5f1e-a0ab-bdb9a22e3846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401959} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8857d6-881c-5549-bde0-3f1a4f61d2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=9\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76d7ebef-b52d-5f30-ac74-d49866c8f6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401956} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5fa6494-6274-595a-88b0-e033638de4a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c02e0e4e-c511-5b9e-9632-6db46dfe1bf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-30052" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-4x+4=9$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2})-4 & =5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-2,q=9,$ and $p+q=7.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2+3=5 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2-3=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=5.$ Thus, the sum is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d78ba1c-5297-5540-afdc-d419d33e0cf7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f24ab63d-b4d8-50ae-8389-5045b51319bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=0\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401963} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1507f7-b1b3-56e3-a38c-1b563d660137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401964} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d7f47d-c746-5735-9e9f-835593e46fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401965} {:answer/id #uuid "aa922213-ad7d-513f-ad50-36362f5e8cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401966} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d50863-24e6-52af-928c-18cf82d1862f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d7f47d-c746-5735-9e9f-835593e46fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401965} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f724395-4532-5650-9bbb-1b5a58b225fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85741874-6828-5db4-a1ab-e8bb9b524df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-30055" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-2x+1=1$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,pq\\,,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot x\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=-1$ and $q=1.$ Thus, $pq=-1.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\ |x-1| & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =1\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-1=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08810676-42bc-5443-a66e-fe0b75d16a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.2\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295309} {:answer/id #uuid "1163523e-ffc1-5258-a832-609cf6ca500a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.2\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295366} {:answer/id #uuid "713eebca-b67d-5424-84ff-32ac69527be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295890} {:answer/id #uuid "97cc87e4-1ea3-5c54-b81a-3581f3f304d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296078} {:answer/id #uuid "f6718237-cd3c-51e1-8d8a-7dd3e545dc5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08810676-42bc-5443-a66e-fe0b75d16a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.2\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295309} :answer-field/id #uuid "a20649a5-6705-5b56-9e09-c13fa8269878" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff7f5106-2da2-5fdb-b8a4-ffffc41203d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-30080" :question/problem "Two cars, $A$ and $B,$ are both initially at the point $O,$ and they both depart from $O$ at the same time. Car $A$ travels due east, and its distance from $O$ is given by $3t\\,\\text{ft}.$ Car $B$ travels due north, and its distance from $O$ is $2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{ft}.$ Here, $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. What is the rate of the change of the distance between the two cars after $2$ seconds?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/aa/aa3f39f65b0785cfca2d1545e4bf331281f523f5e19777372dbb44096bdb4223.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between car $A$ and point $O$,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between car $B$ and point $O$, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between car $A$ and car $B.$\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the distance traveled by the cars after $2$ seconds. We get \n\n$$\nx=x(2)=3(2)=6\\text{ ft}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=y(2)=2(2)^{2}=8\\text{ ft}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 100 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =10\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe velocity of the cars is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(t)=3$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{′}(t)=4t.$ So, when $t=2$ their velocities are\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{′}(2)=8\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds,\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(2)=3\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds.\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(6)(3)+2(8)(8) & =2(10)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 164 & =20\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =8.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the cars is changing at a rate of  $8.2\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0265ab4b-e69c-58cd-bb39-877b73f3298f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,11.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295275} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab493a4-b37f-5582-a8b9-4570920c001f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295861} {:answer/id #uuid "a1678182-8bd9-5b40-83a4-560b3c92dfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdd579a-68d8-530f-aec2-948295be5064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296522} {:answer/id #uuid "f541b6c1-d1ab-5c71-9e34-c29b7b48861a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1678182-8bd9-5b40-83a4-560b3c92dfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0972749-6df3-5836-b156-61b2f017bcaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91d38446-45fe-5f55-b13c-726fa96fa8c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-30269" :question/problem "For the curve $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={1.5}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=2.5({1.5}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2.5\\cdot 1=2.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2.5+8=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.5({1.5}^{0})+8 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 1+8 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,10.5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dbf09c3-18df-50a3-84f7-e6f47d2ef7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295347} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3b22d0-1d8f-5569-b6bf-7b7599fb5580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295876} {:answer/id #uuid "962c31da-ae4b-5022-8358-2aec9b89c95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296067} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e4ad65-9186-54e0-8792-0e53759b7716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296235} {:answer/id #uuid "fa999cda-1369-5935-898a-85571f00711c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e4ad65-9186-54e0-8792-0e53759b7716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296235} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dfec598-1f66-5fc7-8005-3949a52a7718" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60fb4a8e-0b44-516d-9ebb-a8d91494c67e" :question/math-academy-id "q-30368" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=3x+4.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf94b5cf-6407-5931-92df-9f87818e8a71" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045a1df2-5456-559d-b823-6c723a28a7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295287} {:answer/id #uuid "58c766c3-0cc8-5b0f-adf2-345d1dfda737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295781} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea8dabc-09b8-5bbd-b06a-3504111d8ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295878} {:answer/id #uuid "ca41a65d-8d27-5fce-9087-4e74a76cce77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296327} {:answer/id #uuid "e219f4dd-c8b7-5276-9ab5-d9d79f733318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58c766c3-0cc8-5b0f-adf2-345d1dfda737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295781} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b8778a9-6009-5602-aa9a-0b08ab64dd87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be6bd7f3-2e23-5861-b95e-2eb6ae25ed99" :question/math-academy-id "q-30627" :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}s$\nfor $x>-5,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The function $a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1$ is continuous on $[-5,x],$ where $x>-5.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}t & =a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101022 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55fab3ac-cad3-54bd-b07b-9159554b634c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a02b2ac-0bce-55b0-91e4-8c22b954741f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295318} {:answer/id #uuid "235f22ad-40d0-5845-a702-3d539029c35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295459} {:answer/id #uuid "4c89d98a-c3be-5c94-8287-153667e67982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295703} {:answer/id #uuid "bef48e4e-af97-5f81-86d0-5aaaffa9feb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296275} {:answer/id #uuid "d5db9937-d0be-5753-ac50-7bc02f351e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a02b2ac-0bce-55b0-91e4-8c22b954741f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295318} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b70c271-15d7-5977-b27f-aeaabb026c4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32d27667-c963-519c-a8ec-24392218b2f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-30752" :question/problem "What is the average value of $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101022 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55fab3ac-cad3-54bd-b07b-9159554b634c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095165cd-68cc-5092-a541-017374c5c841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295315} {:answer/id #uuid "391a79ba-1514-5b3d-a0fb-45581aae517b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295593} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9c65aa-a057-5380-ab7e-6c41fff6e617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295789} {:answer/id #uuid "841eb217-bc22-5ed1-82f6-7e5a037937c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295974} {:answer/id #uuid "beb18a80-31c2-5374-bb9e-11f4d64b5d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "841eb217-bc22-5ed1-82f6-7e5a037937c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295974} :answer-field/id #uuid "3276f9cb-f55b-5959-8434-6aaa7aa3a121" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72cc8fee-c912-578c-b6ad-a221076e3800" :question/math-academy-id "q-30776" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $g(x)=2{x}^{3}-x-1$ on the interval $[-2,0].$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0-(-2)}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(2{x}^{3}-x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(2{x}^{3}-x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x)|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}-0)-(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{2}-(-2))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-(8-2+2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-8) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e5493f0-49e5-5ac0-a64b-444f26facf5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295811} {:answer/id #uuid "cedf3ffd-b468-57c5-a1fb-5644942d8367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296352} {:answer/id #uuid "d228956c-dabb-5adf-b1f0-eea00077c044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296375} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e90daa-a470-5b92-aeb7-f90fc7f25a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296492} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3169b4-14de-5f3c-837e-5d327959c0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d228956c-dabb-5adf-b1f0-eea00077c044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296375} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef5eb88-b39d-569b-bc4c-949ed152d723" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fda2bca-a959-55aa-9acd-07aede112347" :question/math-academy-id "q-30873" :question/problem "For the curve $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={10}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=1.5({10}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=1.5\\cdot 1=1.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8.5$ units down, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=1.5-8.5=-7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5({10}^{0})-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot 1-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5-8.5 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b340f6-0ac6-5bbf-a7d4-0088ca3c4e5e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07313f55-5d1b-57b8-a58b-0fd2285fb384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295301} {:answer/id #uuid "302be2db-4e95-585b-828f-222678bc2c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295541} {:answer/id #uuid "4e91aacd-fda2-5f11-90aa-6106b854beaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295720} {:answer/id #uuid "6803b977-2128-5c3c-8a0c-1019fb07b1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295852} {:answer/id #uuid "73ac26ff-08d4-5c73-9841-6e57a53e85a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "302be2db-4e95-585b-828f-222678bc2c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295541} :answer-field/id #uuid "942489c8-b11e-5069-8ed0-9bf73a6a722c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68ba7826-02c2-5b69-a594-6dc133f00570" :question/math-academy-id "q-30923" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{x/3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =({e}^{u}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{1}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =e-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19fd901f-e08d-54c5-9cda-68543a3d71a9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b95ba0-8776-5e87-8a07-fb4dd6e251e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{728}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295276} {:answer/id #uuid "36229afc-ab9a-5470-be3f-a37b681ee81b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{364}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295571} {:answer/id #uuid "43ef6c71-98a2-5c12-8945-04c4d08e3cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{364}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295649} {:answer/id #uuid "72e3fae5-ccad-59b0-8fc0-ac0e46bfbfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{364}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295895} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc47e71-ee79-5542-acba-c30ea159db79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72e3fae5-ccad-59b0-8fc0-ac0e46bfbfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{364}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295895} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcf773c9-a379-54a1-a3f0-7de93b756309" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34a4e0e2-73c5-54dc-8277-4607c07e4179" :question/math-academy-id "q-30973" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=6x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of  $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{3}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}){|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(729-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{728}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{364}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eec195c-6549-5de2-b3f0-0417e5f518e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3924a0d6-844d-5dc6-83c6-7db4a2fb4e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<2$ or $x>4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295594} {:answer/id #uuid "440955b3-6ad7-5734-9cd2-e9f88aa0e954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295651} {:answer/id #uuid "77034500-21c2-52e9-9478-7a3d41623c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-4$ or $x>-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295916} {:answer/id #uuid "7798a310-53c1-5f5f-8d13-7e74a6c7123a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4<x<-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295919} {:answer/id #uuid "ef80a80c-3c32-544a-b833-12476f1cf9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "440955b3-6ad7-5734-9cd2-e9f88aa0e954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295651} :answer-field/id #uuid "41198266-3a32-5b6f-aeaf-82b61bfcb718" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b9f08f5-9d21-5599-bebd-c942241b0925" :question/math-academy-id "q-31015" :question/problem "![](images/11/1126d885ff5000b4afdadffef7b47dec3b2932322dfa7b75d16bc00993218a0e.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x-2)(x-4)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x-2)(x-4)>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-(x-2)(x-4)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc39c970e292e38519e609367c9f4a3d810aaca99e7040bb6227ff45401b2efc.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $2<x<4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bb394c3-cd7c-523d-88df-b1306d9d528f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c95a3fca-97ff-5e46-ad67-42c5b5b68ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bc4c0c21c45fc3ed02d88150facf89718896a3e2992105f5a8a5d89b0355f9e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390594} {:answer/id #uuid "73326b1a-1067-5996-991e-bb9d853a4e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6edb90966ce90a141e3fa4ee1a9eb3cf9c7131e3121e45f56b16bc4395076d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390595} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a74885-a855-53a6-a12b-5c1f9db59688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/062274f402396a61c76fae3a47c54de80ad31deac970c746f3f8f4627ce535f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390596} {:answer/id #uuid "dbeafe1a-8a14-5f3a-8d70-6e398d0ebc6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b3/b3f96cf981a9c127dd0e3c0fa3b73f548db8c640bbc4f0e048b4576356b69532.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390597} {:answer/id #uuid "44eec2a2-c1e5-5830-a6f1-3345cbd3b0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b48f88ac53107fddece776c81d91fc5684c7d6f06ea3b862fb99cd973eae2dc3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73326b1a-1067-5996-991e-bb9d853a4e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6edb90966ce90a141e3fa4ee1a9eb3cf9c7131e3121e45f56b16bc4395076d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390595} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e7cf18-216c-59ac-8255-427849d7152c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6a50d86-9c2e-59fc-b26e-b4043bba0c39" :question/math-academy-id "q-31146" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}-4?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/e2/e2f13711ca62a8a4a698e23bdfe78caca6df44d88ded27b88c517219dffbb866.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $4$ units down. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=-4,$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,-3).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}-4$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc7f93220ac18ac5671b7b41de141940ca5296139b836d56755fdccf6164ed26.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral Given the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1c39ec3-887e-50dc-8edd-3cdaf54b45c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x-1+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390601} {:answer/id #uuid "37a1fcc8-9234-5641-b7e1-5e7f084efe44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390602} {:answer/id #uuid "2654cacd-7b30-583d-bda0-799991c8b3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390603} {:answer/id #uuid "9c69b59d-0c96-50b5-ba49-1c3d2aa4e633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(2{x}^{2}+x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390604} {:answer/id #uuid "ab719555-3b8a-5291-ac4c-1377d5597894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x-1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37a1fcc8-9234-5641-b7e1-5e7f084efe44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390602} :answer-field/id #uuid "199eb7c0-d99d-5255-b218-c8c2cfb6234c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c69be2ea-7b70-598d-bb9a-251384a82770" :question/math-academy-id "q-3121" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a87ae2b230e66b685051ee7d13987f5f68690a07a67ade9e7af0eb870f6224f.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=x+1,$ as shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/e7/e77170aac6d81b1cfd0e93804b0c59802f28089d24aeaff065da04cabc44c172.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(x+1)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096624 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59a296c-f26b-57d2-9f66-3089d690018b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-6$ and $x =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095238} {:answer/id #uuid "448169d1-793a-5de6-ab93-2ab928cd2db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-4$ and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095239} {:answer/id #uuid "4959333a-5e8c-57af-a996-2eb528af419a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095240} {:answer/id #uuid "645b2ac0-0d2d-51c4-983b-5d9739a24c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095241} {:answer/id #uuid "7efb67cf-1659-5361-bf87-0b82d0524c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-4$, $x = 0$, and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095242}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d1936dc6-d067-5933-92a7-774acfe3f044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095237} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a3b2c4a-a63b-5807-aacc-5245a5e6b02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401969} {:answer/id #uuid "46b9e8a3-14f5-5f69-831f-e7e0d797a1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401970} {:answer/id #uuid "c082a9b0-d023-52a6-be52-56d8828805a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401971} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3248ac-1443-5057-98d3-f07c503f35c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401972} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0e922d-f9cb-5465-99c6-9d8aecf68d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46b9e8a3-14f5-5f69-831f-e7e0d797a1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401970} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ff50305-88e5-5409-b5ae-8fb7fea82cf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05af1cea-c88c-5fcc-8838-0055bd7f29aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-31213" :question/problem "![](images/19/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=0$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2125528a-4464-51f1-a6cf-1a62208a5272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295453} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd57e48-e096-5a21-9e35-d893d06eff4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295520} {:answer/id #uuid "8815930d-5d3e-5b84-bba5-2121ddcd2334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0)$ and $(4,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296001} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b8440e-84c6-5661-8c06-2d43e89202e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0)$ and $(0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296217} {:answer/id #uuid "bc781f14-e709-5a2a-be21-fc63c1ca70ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc781f14-e709-5a2a-be21-fc63c1ca70ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296259} :answer-field/id #uuid "8006db89-19e4-548d-9078-4a9c40044888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f627add3-7eef-5ed1-b10b-a3822778f5e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-31214" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9fe57341b631d34a9dfa41619a29f816b00b41b3b07f450fd333808230835e2c.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,4)$ only. \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(0,4)$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ccdca2-40a0-51dd-83ba-2344bbec440f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295642} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc84882-5388-58ec-a566-fb30b7f6ab42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296035} {:answer/id #uuid "b149287d-31cb-5f98-b59f-e2b247461334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(-2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296201} {:answer/id #uuid "cfcfc15c-184c-56e4-9509-e3be8619bae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296357} {:answer/id #uuid "fad2dba1-d0ca-5773-8549-1415bbf003a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(2,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41ccdca2-40a0-51dd-83ba-2344bbec440f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295642} :answer-field/id #uuid "fba441ab-ea13-5287-b1e0-537ff6daa6c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2faeba0-b737-514d-8d25-1eceee0628d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-31228" :question/problem "![](images/30/305fa76994ff0be3c43894226eaa82d3d9e016c6add11cd48d80486c50643ed0.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ on the interval $(-4,-2)$ only. Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(-4,-2)$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f9548e6-28ef-5a97-ab04-dfb1b6e23c16" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfff078-9fb4-5b67-bb26-7811957a40b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{ln⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295341} {:answer/id #uuid "36c4e4d8-ea74-510a-8195-fcca114fa7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295573} {:answer/id #uuid "4d71c1ee-dea6-5890-90dd-6cf50a8cd3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295711} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3c34e1-3b8b-568c-889c-57fe85dd59ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296256} {:answer/id #uuid "cd13943f-d3e1-5920-9b15-4d7240e4db43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc3c34e1-3b8b-568c-889c-57fe85dd59ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296256} :answer-field/id #uuid "b94705d1-73b6-521f-a07d-12831457766a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f474ed40-025d-552e-9e67-da66415ffc42" :question/math-academy-id "q-31245" :question/problem "$\\int -2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Factoring out the constant $-2$ and calculating the resulting integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddda862-700e-5259-8a9b-324825fe885d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295436} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3aafd2-10f8-5647-aeef-217f0b123766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295606} {:answer/id #uuid "4aaae1a6-c031-5bc5-a104-23401003f961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295691} {:answer/id #uuid "531024e5-c103-583c-92eb-e4200ac7a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}-\\frac{{3}^{2x}}{ln⁡x}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295744} {:answer/id #uuid "56637843-7c20-5243-80d5-7c1405ee9de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b3aafd2-10f8-5647-aeef-217f0b123766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295606} :answer-field/id #uuid "32505d16-6c49-5e59-9ed0-fdcb1b385a52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42f6b48b-f5fd-5245-b823-ea76a025e943" :question/math-academy-id "q-31252" :question/problem "$\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb56f0a-ead0-5794-9e33-7fe0e1f87dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136ad750-eb9e-5055-a26a-c8cda8dba5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295374} {:answer/id #uuid "1cce689b-d66b-5419-b42f-1c935770579a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295433} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7cbd2b-769e-5c2a-8198-1408890da6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295702} {:answer/id #uuid "be094b01-e8a4-5075-a548-43ad86b83a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296269} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c1681e-054f-5ee6-ac7d-f24a14f0c567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0c1681e-054f-5ee6-ac7d-f24a14f0c567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296463} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed22d9b2-1f65-5896-a2a0-0c98a0de1f14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "683c5003-d2ff-5388-892e-f05923834b32" :question/math-academy-id "q-31283" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={x}^{2}-6csc⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-6csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2x-6(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =2x+6csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa28f5c4-135e-576a-baa0-68c41b5139f6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing Other Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097bf9be-35d0-5117-aeeb-185f65a4e444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295316} {:answer/id #uuid "0c595f3e-4a64-5335-bfee-ef4f9c199d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295335} {:answer/id #uuid "3a9d8072-eeac-5190-ac53-423bbc5c0d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295602} {:answer/id #uuid "7c854fd3-91d0-5ade-bfec-4aa656855a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295948} {:answer/id #uuid "943f1ef0-98c7-561c-8c98-1ab90aa0f4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "943f1ef0-98c7-561c-8c98-1ab90aa0f4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296059} :answer-field/id #uuid "94521d82-e55e-520c-aa39-65e9dae6d07e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc1950d7-cc9c-5e9a-8f86-5dcc05de2e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-31329" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {log}_{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074a9ec9-6bfa-51c3-b293-a2e6c7634c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295302} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8e6cf9-9f11-5e02-a08b-b0ce3fd9f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295608} {:answer/id #uuid "64d5cac3-e49f-55a4-a517-b40d5927cff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295841} {:answer/id #uuid "748fff53-6b97-5ddb-a2b7-7573ce8fffb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295904} {:answer/id #uuid "c09b0310-1b57-58cd-bbcf-bbf81a785509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b8e6cf9-9f11-5e02-a08b-b0ce3fd9f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295608} :answer-field/id #uuid "67494433-5316-509e-bbfc-ae0d8a99e4af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d5db746-ab9b-5b5a-bc25-31e21fc4632d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31338" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\pi -3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2\\pi -3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143d951a-1e7a-598c-ab01-6ac4eebb972d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295381} {:answer/id #uuid "80ac022d-2fa2-5312-b6bb-20edccc427c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{cot}^{3}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295959} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd53f52-57a0-58c2-84e5-51e7836dcaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{2}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296358} {:answer/id #uuid "df819a99-e827-5e8d-8038-4bdc1f81e2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296451} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c37201-fff5-5a86-9be9-3c9d741d2eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "143d951a-1e7a-598c-ab01-6ac4eebb972d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295381} :answer-field/id #uuid "c18783dd-cacf-52c8-94df-17f7aaad8258" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60550674-e8b4-5f20-9b57-5b71c82e6894" :question/math-academy-id "q-31361" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(5x-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=5x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1))\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5(0)-1) \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-20(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa28f5c4-135e-576a-baa0-68c41b5139f6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing Other Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a00bd77-f937-55b8-9148-a5c1a2df5750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295597} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0e44e3-51df-57b7-9de3-73290c5703d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295690} {:answer/id #uuid "8b673669-26b9-55f4-aeb7-78ad83c0d37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296009} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b0c7ae-0328-590a-8a7c-83c5e5868a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296290} {:answer/id #uuid "e1684df1-979f-5851-a0c3-4dbc8f47887a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}^{2}⁡x}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1b0c7ae-0328-590a-8a7c-83c5e5868a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296290} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0f353b4-b1a0-534f-8162-8c7bfcdf5cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0501bb6-8d0d-5290-9b3d-4f26ccd12ac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-31394" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {log}_{5}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41b7d83f-3b9b-556c-9e49-13df9c3b597e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dae15a2-1ee3-5e1c-9740-88dbcb27308a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295346} {:answer/id #uuid "21c52b8f-99f3-501e-b48b-11fa940e47fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{ln}^{3}⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295455} {:answer/id #uuid "8339edfa-ac01-5a35-ba61-f72d13beb9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295971} {:answer/id #uuid "afde279f-b6c5-5153-a7ee-1486db54540c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}{ln}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296193} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf9ca14-d6e0-59ec-bd95-95f8ca1bf1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\sqrt{{ln}^{3}⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21c52b8f-99f3-501e-b48b-11fa940e47fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{ln}^{3}⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295455} :answer-field/id #uuid "15bc6d3f-2f78-5fec-acf5-1f972668822a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7cf9e06a-68e3-56b4-9085-33ef355832a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-31398" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{ln}^{3}⁡x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa28f5c4-135e-576a-baa0-68c41b5139f6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing Other Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27327c99-efb7-58e8-a74e-787cf861a36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{log}_{2}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295482} {:answer/id #uuid "87c24d2f-2c74-5040-90ac-f36195ad44b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{log}_{3}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295999} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd76f53-ed5e-50ea-9b0a-4825cbde5b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x{log}_{3}^{2}⁡x}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296097} {:answer/id #uuid "cee39ee1-4f54-5380-b841-9cb4997a9fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}){log}_{3}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296353} {:answer/id #uuid "e80a0ef7-8043-5002-b2dc-df0cdf73346a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{log}_{3}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87c24d2f-2c74-5040-90ac-f36195ad44b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{log}_{3}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295999} :answer-field/id #uuid "91e8e1d5-6ca1-583b-a916-cb7fac5a8d3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8e9ea16-ddaf-5f82-ac82-079325b04f6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-31401" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}).$Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\int u\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{log}_{3}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa28f5c4-135e-576a-baa0-68c41b5139f6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing Other Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26cf2f12-d9f3-59fd-be22-70990f0c064c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295477} {:answer/id #uuid "32474011-c0eb-5c98-b028-863b8f17da19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295550} {:answer/id #uuid "8b40389e-34eb-5524-b04f-3adc8016457b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5})\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡(3x)|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296008} {:answer/id #uuid "a5006fea-9435-5ebd-a15e-7ec4fb282356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡(3x)|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296137} {:answer/id #uuid "e0893163-fbac-5102-8d54-6ed75f43ac2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡(3x)|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0893163-fbac-5102-8d54-6ed75f43ac2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡(3x)|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296461} :answer-field/id #uuid "c59c5853-4a44-56f0-94bb-09e77fa03053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f906b7ab-49ff-57e2-8ffb-323870b5ae05" :question/math-academy-id "q-31402" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{x{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡(3x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡(3x)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19fd901f-e08d-54c5-9cda-68543a3d71a9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e3c76b3-9a7e-5c31-b43e-02f9be81294d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295533} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee811f8-8af9-53fa-be26-778f4f77cd67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{5}^{-x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295722} {:answer/id #uuid "57a67b0f-0c05-5b5a-b5ea-792d6c56a440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295773} {:answer/id #uuid "5de84312-4ff6-5203-8c07-5a2dbc5c4c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295809} {:answer/id #uuid "c521dc80-7e50-540f-93b7-e321d21cdb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e3c76b3-9a7e-5c31-b43e-02f9be81294d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295533} :answer-field/id #uuid "4944a858-6895-5891-aed1-aabff8de8a8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91e61cac-e373-57ad-92b8-00ce9bccf78c" :question/math-academy-id "q-31412" :question/problem "$\\int {5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=-x$, then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {5}^{u}\\cdot (-1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int {5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19fd901f-e08d-54c5-9cda-68543a3d71a9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1277c0e2-5004-5ee3-b7fe-7022e68c6a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{6}^{x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295368} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdefd48-2695-5a38-8f5d-ed070355571e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{6}^{x/3}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295950} {:answer/id #uuid "cd384b9e-3f21-5c93-8eef-f209d288044d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{x/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296343} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ca43b4-033c-5cd0-a8a6-8ea7eb292ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296410} {:answer/id #uuid "e19fa8a4-2ede-5cc9-a74c-dbb6cdba1380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{6}^{x/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd384b9e-3f21-5c93-8eef-f209d288044d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{x/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296343} :answer-field/id #uuid "1db9f319-6a3e-51d1-bbc2-fd789260b0da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a2ea8a4-727a-5496-81cd-8ee505de6239" :question/math-academy-id "q-31417" :question/problem "$\\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{3},$ then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {6}^{u}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {6}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\frac{{6}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{x/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184350 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8720b44-317d-590e-a251-bfcec97e0513" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82507a0e-9640-5f91-baac-ab6e9c5da3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{8}^{3x}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295968} {:answer/id #uuid "90098dd5-289f-547e-b363-0c0f0a9af66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{8}^{3{x}^{2}}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296038} {:answer/id #uuid "b0881961-d42a-52bd-baff-db91f2644d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{8}^{3{x}^{2}}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296198} {:answer/id #uuid "bd10d05c-0dcf-50d3-bed0-8065b29b1c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{8}^{3{x}^{2}}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296263} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd37029-5f4f-5d4c-b32f-36f0f45b0933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{8}^{3{x}^{2}}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddd37029-5f4f-5d4c-b32f-36f0f45b0933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{8}^{3{x}^{2}}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296446} :answer-field/id #uuid "f130d306-f973-5ac4-a801-416349e0b6ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a383a3e5-774c-580f-ac7a-6d64870e5f9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-31420" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int x{8}^{3{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u=3{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{8}^{3{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {8}^{3{x}^{2}}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {8}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int {8}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\frac{{8}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{8}^{3{x}^{2}}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-fie